9
00:00:57,280 --> 00:01:02,160
With me today, Leon Sassen, co-founder and CTO at Rise Science.

10
00:01:02,560 --> 00:01:17,520
On the podcast, I talk with Leon about why web onboarding should sell the problem instead of the solution, how discounted paid trials are beating free trials, and why creative that flopped for app ads might just crush it for web funnels.

11
00:01:18,320 --> 00:01:20,880
Hey, Leon, thanks for joining me again on the podcast.

12
00:01:21,520 --> 00:01:22,720
Hey, David, good to be here.

13
00:01:22,880 --> 00:01:26,720
It's been a minute since last time and the RevenueCat conference.

14
00:01:27,680 --> 00:01:28,240
Good times since.

15
00:01:28,880 --> 00:01:30,240
Yeah, always good to chat with you.

16
00:01:30,640 --> 00:01:33,840
And the topic of web is something you and I have talked a lot about over the years.

17
00:01:34,080 --> 00:01:35,360
You were one of the early folks.

18
00:01:35,840 --> 00:01:44,160
I mean, I remember talking with you at MAU, gosh, four years ago or something about Stripe and chargebacks and like the challenges you were having.

19
00:01:44,320 --> 00:01:45,440
So you've been around the block.

20
00:01:45,680 --> 00:01:49,440
And I know in the last year you've found some things that are finally really clicking.

21
00:01:49,680 --> 00:01:54,720
And so I was excited to have you on to talk about that and like what's working and why it's working.

22
00:01:54,960 --> 00:01:57,440
And I think you're super introspective and super into.

23
00:01:58,240 --> 00:01:58,320
data.

24
00:01:58,880 --> 00:02:01,360
And so I felt like we're going to get a really fresh perspective.

25
00:02:01,520 --> 00:02:04,240
So tell me, what's working and why is it working?

26
00:02:04,720 --> 00:02:06,800
Oh man, it depends who you ask.

27
00:02:06,960 --> 00:02:26,400
But yeah, we've been working on, we've been trying to work on web stuff for a long, long time, probably in earnest around the end of 2021, early 2022, when the whole IDFA kind of ATT scan period of time that it kind of wrecked everything for everyone.

28
00:02:27,040 --> 00:02:30,400
So it was a time of people really and us too freaking out about it, right?

29
00:02:30,560 --> 00:02:31,440
What's the future, right?

30
00:02:31,680 --> 00:02:33,360
Like, is mobile marketing dead?

31
00:02:33,680 --> 00:02:35,920
Because for a few months it was totally dead.

32
00:02:36,560 --> 00:02:41,520
And the obvious first order thing to do was like, okay, let's do web funnels.

33
00:02:41,680 --> 00:02:43,280
And that was, you know, early 2002.

34
00:02:43,360 --> 00:02:45,440
So you kind of tried that a few times.

35
00:02:45,760 --> 00:02:50,480
And back then, I would say there were some large players in the space doing it.

36
00:02:50,960 --> 00:02:57,280
It was more e commerce, but you still had some, maybe like Noom was the biggest web funnel at the time, as I believe.

37
00:02:57,440 --> 00:02:59,920
So back then, I'll start with everything that wasn't working.

38
00:03:00,080 --> 00:03:03,360
So we did that for a while and we were like, okay, we need to figure out web.

39
00:03:03,760 --> 00:03:04,720
And that was pretty naive.

40
00:03:04,960 --> 00:03:08,160
Hey, let's rebuild our app onboarding in web, right?

41
00:03:08,480 --> 00:03:10,080
That's kind of, okay, let's do that.

42
00:03:10,400 --> 00:03:10,800
We did that.

43
00:03:11,120 --> 00:03:11,840
It didn't quite work.

44
00:03:12,400 --> 00:03:14,240
Did that again, maybe six months later.

45
00:03:14,480 --> 00:03:17,280
We just couldn't get it to work, like not even close.

46
00:03:17,760 --> 00:03:30,080
And we kind of gave up and we kind of scratched it as like, hey, our app relies too much on health git data and all these cool features that are deep integrations on your phone.

47
00:03:30,240 --> 00:03:38,080
So maybe that's why it doesn't work on the web because we don't have your health git data and we don't have all these deep integrations with your wearables and your sleep schedule and all that.

48
00:03:38,240 --> 00:03:39,920
So we kind of parked that project.

49
00:03:40,320 --> 00:03:45,440
And also at that point, all the marketing world started working a little better on app.

50
00:03:45,840 --> 00:03:59,040
So the whole IDFA apocalypse, it was definitely bad, but things were covered and the big published, you know, like Meta and Google and just everyone kind of fe figured out how to work with the new system, even though it was painful, there was a way forward.

51
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:04,000
So we kind of kept going along working on core, you know, making our app better and focusing entirely on our app.

52
00:04:04,480 --> 00:04:11,840
And really, maybe in 2024 or so, so now two years ago, we're like, right, let's give web another chance.

53
00:04:12,240 --> 00:04:14,240
And we never gave up truly.

54
00:04:14,480 --> 00:04:18,240
It was more about, hey, now it's on time, but we know it's a big opportunity.

55
00:04:18,640 --> 00:04:28,480
And maybe the naive take, and maybe that was our third attempt was, hey, it still needs to look similar to the app, and it's helpful to kind of like bypass some of the Apple fees or whatnot.

56
00:04:28,960 --> 00:04:33,840
Very quickly kind of talking to some people and learning, it was like, right, those two things are pretty wrong..

57
00:04:34,480 --> 00:04:41,920
And first of all, it changes the dynamics so much that the Apple billing thing is totally almost comparing like apples to oranges.

58
00:04:42,080 --> 00:04:44,320
It's not even remotely the equivalent formula.

59
00:04:44,480 --> 00:04:46,800
Rather, it's just one small piece of the equation.

60
00:04:47,200 --> 00:05:03,520
And second, once we talked to a few people that have cracked it and kind of coached us along the way and then kind of put us on the right path, we've also learned that the way web funnels are successful tend to be very different than the way app onboarding flows tend to be successful.

61
00:05:03,680 --> 00:05:07,200
And they're almost like fundamentally different parts of the user journey.

62
00:05:07,440 --> 00:05:08,720
So you have to treat them like that.

63
00:05:09,120 --> 00:05:24,320
So that kind of started really, I mean, hundreds and hundreds of iterations to get the right mechanism of what's going to work in web is different than app, and we should treat it as, hey, it's a different surface, a different channel entirely from the marketing side all the way to user experience.

64
00:05:24,560 --> 00:05:28,640
So we kind of started from scratch about this is not at all related to the app, right?

65
00:05:28,960 --> 00:05:33,200
This is a web funnel, and let's make that work unrelated to what works on the app.

66
00:05:33,520 --> 00:05:38,880
And that kind of started a learning path that, so far, has been super promising and super productive.

67
00:05:39,040 --> 00:05:41,440
So happy to dive in in any areas there.

68
00:05:41,920 --> 00:05:42,000
Yeah.

69
00:05:42,160 --> 00:05:43,360
Well, let's get specific.

70
00:05:43,600 --> 00:05:48,080
When you say you need to treat it as a whole separate channel, what were the learnings?

71
00:05:48,320 --> 00:05:52,480
Like, what, why is the app onboarding not working on the web.

72
00:05:53,040 --> 00:05:56,000
And then how do you make that web specific onboarding?

73
00:05:56,160 --> 00:06:05,760
Like what is it that people are enticed by and resonating with on the web that doesn't work in app and why does it work in app and not on the web?

74
00:06:06,240 --> 00:06:33,920
Yeah, the thing that we learned on our side and this might kind of vary by different developers and different companies, but one of the things that was pretty obvious from the get-go was the audience, especially if you look at it from the lens of, hey, you're acquiring users through kind of paid marketing or kind of like any at scale marketing channels, the audience that you often attract to a web funnel tends to be very different than the audience that you attract to an app funnel.

75
00:06:34,160 --> 00:06:39,040
And there's obviously a little bit of overlap because at the end of the day, the fundamental core user problem might be similar.

76
00:06:39,200 --> 00:06:43,200
In our case, it's like people that might be exhausted in their lives and are looking for a solution, right?

77
00:06:43,440 --> 00:06:54,000
That's a very kind of overarching theme, but the audience is so different that we found some marketing channels down to the level of because the campaigns tend to be web campaigns, not app campaigns.

78
00:06:54,400 --> 00:07:00,560
You tend to have a chance to attract customers and users that are not traditional app users.

79
00:07:01,040 --> 00:07:08,080
So they're not the type of users that are going to instinctively download an app as a solution to random problems in their lives, right?

80
00:07:08,320 --> 00:07:09,120
Whereas these are users.

81
00:07:09,760 --> 00:07:17,360
that would happily buy stuff online, they're engaged in e commerce and they're happy to take a peek at something and go deeper.

82
00:07:17,680 --> 00:07:22,400
And if the solution happens to involve an app, you might be able to convince them that this is the right thing for them.

83
00:07:22,560 --> 00:07:29,440
But these are not users that would traditionally end up in the sort of the bottom funnel, hey, I'm going to download an app type audience.

84
00:07:29,760 --> 00:07:47,440
So it's a lot more higher, you know, top of funnel audience, which means that a lot of what ends up working is treating it as a lower intent to start, but often high willingness to pay because on some of these marketing channels, you are attracting essentially an e commerce audience, depending how you do it.

85
00:07:47,920 --> 00:07:55,680
So it it ends up being because the audience was different and and, and, you know, we can get into behavioral traits of the audience, but even down to demographics, the basic thing, right?

86
00:07:55,920 --> 00:08:10,320
Like you might be able to attract, we found that on web funnels, we can attract a lot of older audiences, like forty, fifty, sixty year old people at scale that maybe the app, you know, the app, the traditional app marketing wasn't really able to reach them.

87
00:08:10,640 --> 00:08:11,600
Who knows exactly why?

88
00:08:12,080 --> 00:08:18,560
And then you can imagine very quickly that the way that you talk to a fifty year old is very different than the way we talk to a thirty year old.

89
00:08:18,880 --> 00:08:21,680
So especially in the lens of sleep and health, right?

90
00:08:21,840 --> 00:08:23,360
Like the problems might be very, very different.

91
00:08:23,680 --> 00:08:29,520
So because the audience changes, then you're starting essentially with, we'll be a whole new blank slate on what works there.

92
00:08:29,760 --> 00:08:34,560
So that's pretty high level, but it was pretty shocking that, hey, the audience is different.

93
00:08:35,280 --> 00:08:44,400
And that brings a flip side to the, or a corollary to the marketing that is because the audience is different, then the creative that works can be very different on the web than app.

94
00:08:44,800 --> 00:09:01,440
Not all of it, but some of it can be very different, which then means that in a way, Web Funnel, when you're running at scale on, say, Meta or Google or whatever other channel you might be running, you can end up where you have almost like uncorrelated marketing performance between different types of campaigns, right?

95
00:09:01,680 --> 00:09:06,960
Like where you can have, you know, everyone that markets might tell you, oh, this month per performance is down.

96
00:09:07,120 --> 00:09:08,080
Everyone's freaking out.

97
00:09:08,320 --> 00:09:15,040
And when you run the web and app separately, you realize, like, oh, maybe the web is down this month, but app is up.

98
00:09:15,440 --> 00:09:16,240
Or the other way around.

99
00:09:16,560 --> 00:09:26,720
So you end up with a actually very different sort of cycle that in a way just makes the ecosystem a lot healthier and more robust to flux and performance.

100
00:09:26,960 --> 00:09:30,320
So that's a bit of the high level that we've learned on the website.

101
00:09:30,880 --> 00:09:37,920
Are there specific channels or even placements that you feel like do especially well on the web that you're seeing a lot of success with?

102
00:09:38,160 --> 00:09:42,800
So on Meta, is it more the Facebook placements than the Instagram placements?

103
00:09:42,960 --> 00:09:48,560
Or is it Google versus meta, like, are you seeing any specific channels perform better than others?

104
00:09:49,040 --> 00:09:54,880
We find that generally speaking, they all work the same because the web audience, at least for us, has leaned older.

105
00:09:55,520 --> 00:10:02,880
You tend to see more shift towards Facebook Blue app than Instagram, but that's pretty high level and that ebbs and flows all the time depending on creative.

106
00:10:03,360 --> 00:10:14,000
So on that side, there's not a lot of differences, but on the creative side, we often see creative that maybe wasn't working on app that now starts working on web and vice versa.

107
00:10:14,080 --> 00:10:21,440
So you have more chances of winning when you can try something twice and maybe it's going to work on one side and not the other because the audience is different.

108
00:10:21,760 --> 00:10:37,840
So our job on the web is we're getting people to realize the problem they have, which often is something that rhymes with, hey, you're exhausted or you're, you know, you have all these potential problems in your life and you might not have realized that sleep is the reason why you have those problems.

109
00:10:38,080 --> 00:10:43,360
So we have this app that will help you kind of take control and fill up better and kind of unlock that part of your life.

110
00:10:43,920 --> 00:10:55,520
So the web funnel ends up sort of, we had to unlearn a lot of the app onboarding that we spent a lot of time crafting, which was on the app, it was a lot about time to value.

111
00:10:55,680 --> 00:10:57,840
Hey, get to time to value, get to the app moment.

112
00:10:58,320 --> 00:11:08,320
You have as long as you want, but you really have to almost like impress the user and give them value before you kind of ask for a hard commitment of a trial start or credit card or whatever.

113
00:11:08,480 --> 00:11:23,760
On web, we realize that because people tend to be much higher on the consideration phase, often you're just trying to go deeper on understanding the problem and personalizing it, but not going straight into solutions because they might not be sold on the solution yet.

114
00:11:24,320 --> 00:11:30,400
So just going out and trying to tell them their plan and tell them all the things that the features are going to do, that might not work.

115
00:11:30,720 --> 00:11:34,800
Whereas trying to understand and make them realize, like, oh, this is the problem you have.

116
00:11:35,040 --> 00:11:37,280
Let's now talk to solutions later on the app.

117
00:11:37,600 --> 00:11:53,680
And it's all like a separate phase and it's about helping them realize that they might have a problem and that we could have a solution for them that they should try out versus trying to go straight to the moment of oh wow, these people are going to solve my problem, you know, on the first thirty seconds because they're just higher in the consideration phase.

118
00:11:53,920 --> 00:11:56,720
They they just haven't downloaded the app, tend to be a different audience.

119
00:11:56,880 --> 00:11:59,680
So we did have to unlearn a lot of that product side.

120
00:12:00,240 --> 00:12:06,080
It actually makes a lot of sense when you put it that way that when you download an app, you're looking for a solution.

121
00:12:06,400 --> 00:12:09,920
And so the app onboarding is to get you to a solution.

122
00:12:10,960 --> 00:12:20,000
When you see an ad on the web and you're on a website, like I know when I'm like surfing the web or like reading about something, I'm I'm more in the discovery phase.

123
00:12:20,160 --> 00:12:20,880
Like what is it about?

124
00:12:21,280 --> 00:12:22,320
my sleep that's broken?

125
00:12:22,640 --> 00:12:23,280
What do I like?

126
00:12:23,600 --> 00:12:24,160
What do I need?

127
00:12:24,560 --> 00:12:36,080
And so it kind of makes sense that the web onboarding needs that more kind of exploratory framing the problem much deeper than the app where people are just ready for the solution.

128
00:12:36,560 --> 00:12:47,840
Would you say that kind of perspective also informs creative or like, is there any pattern between the creative that's working on the web versus what's working with app campaigns that you're sending into the app?

129
00:12:48,400 --> 00:12:48,960
There probably are.

130
00:12:49,280 --> 00:12:50,480
We just don't think about it that way.

131
00:12:50,640 --> 00:12:53,120
We don't think about like, oh, web creative versus app creative.

132
00:12:53,280 --> 00:12:59,600
We think about our creative as a, you know, much more deep into what customer personas we're trying to reach, what hooks.

133
00:13:00,080 --> 00:13:02,240
and value props we're trying to sell.

134
00:13:02,880 --> 00:13:06,480
And yes, because the audience is different, but the creative team doesn't really care.

135
00:13:06,720 --> 00:13:11,280
It just happens that they get a different persona or a different type of audience that they're working on the creative.

136
00:13:11,440 --> 00:13:14,240
We don't necessarily try crafting creative for web.

137
00:13:14,560 --> 00:13:20,160
It's more like, hey, I don't know, web has more females over fifty years old, so there's more creative for that audience, let's say.

138
00:13:20,400 --> 00:13:23,520
It doesn't matter if it's app or web, at least for our current status.

139
00:13:23,920 --> 00:13:35,840
So then are you trying most creatives on both app and web simultaneously and just letting the algorithms decide what wins, or are you creating web specific campaigns and app specific campaigns?

140
00:13:36,080 --> 00:13:36,880
So that's a deep question.

141
00:13:37,200 --> 00:13:38,560
That's very, very deep creative testing.

142
00:13:39,040 --> 00:13:44,560
at scale, I feel is the never ending, there's always a hack, always a system, everyone thinks they're better.

143
00:13:44,720 --> 00:13:47,360
Facebook tells you or Meta or Google, they all tell you what to do.

144
00:13:47,440 --> 00:13:51,760
And then the experts tell you not to do what the other people tell you to do.

145
00:13:51,920 --> 00:13:53,120
So you have to try it all.

146
00:13:53,280 --> 00:13:54,640
And I feel every six months, things change.

147
00:13:55,120 --> 00:14:03,200
But one of the nice things about web is the algorithm that learns for the delivery tends to be a lot.

148
00:14:03,600 --> 00:14:21,280
So the algorithm, the signal and the measurement is a lot more effective because the platforms, Meta, Google don't and TikTok and whatever else you may be, they don't have to deal with the kind of scan, ATT, privacy related constraints that, hey, data is delayed, attribution is broken.

149
00:14:21,440 --> 00:14:25,440
You get only see like 50% of the traffic instead of 100%.

150
00:14:25,680 --> 00:14:37,200
So on the web, because it's more traditional from the lens of the advertising platform, web is essentially an e-commerce, which is a lot easier to pipe all the instrumentation into the system.

151
00:14:37,520 --> 00:14:38,880
So the learning system is faster.

152
00:14:39,040 --> 00:14:40,800
So it's a lot easier to run those campaigns.

153
00:14:41,040 --> 00:14:41,120
One.

154
00:14:41,760 --> 00:14:44,640
And then two, the algorithm tends to optimize a lot faster.

155
00:14:45,280 --> 00:14:48,720
So you tend to find the people a lot faster.

156
00:14:48,880 --> 00:14:58,800
And you tend to know if creative works a lot faster than on app where you might not be able to like really dive down and you might need to wake a week or two to know to know if it's working and depends on the budget.

157
00:14:58,880 --> 00:15:00,560
If it's too low budget, it might be longer.

158
00:15:00,640 --> 00:15:07,120
So on the web, a lot of that iteration cycle tends to be much, much, much faster, which is a huge unlock for the web.

159
00:15:07,520 --> 00:15:09,760
So it's not necessarily about testing strategy.

160
00:15:10,480 --> 00:15:19,280
We do test a lot of most creative across both app and web separately, but at the beginning, I believe we may not even have been doing that.

161
00:15:19,520 --> 00:15:22,720
So it depends on testing strategy and we're always iterating on that.

162
00:15:23,520 --> 00:15:28,000
I wanted to dive into a few tactical things since we are running short of time.

163
00:15:28,800 --> 00:15:31,440
Are you spinning up a bunch of different funnels?

164
00:15:31,600 --> 00:15:34,320
Like, is it like, are you matching the funnel to the creative?

165
00:15:34,640 --> 00:15:36,000
Like, you know, is it a little bit more creative?

166
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:44,880
Is there a kind of persona bent where they do a creative with a specific persona in mind, and then that leads to a very specific funnel with that persona in mind?

167
00:15:45,200 --> 00:15:58,240
Yeah, I think that's the holy grail of web funnels that you can actually go to the level of the creative itself matches the funnel in a way that app campaigns just can't do at all because the way you know, you have to download the app in the middle of the flow.

168
00:15:58,800 --> 00:16:00,000
We do that to some extent.

169
00:16:00,160 --> 00:16:08,320
It hasn't been a huge breakthrough yet because it does require an order of magnitude more complex kind of operations between marketing and product and coordination of everything.

170
00:16:08,720 --> 00:16:17,280
But we are seeing very interesting success that you can imagine where you can have very different creatives and funnels for very different personas and it's all customized.

171
00:16:17,440 --> 00:16:32,080
So we have some level of customization, but we're going much deeper on that because it does seem that it's going to be another kind of huge wave of unlocking when you can truly personalize the experience for that audience that they're coming from a very specific type of user need.

172
00:16:32,320 --> 00:16:38,160
So for example, there's people that are tired and want to sleep better because they want to be more productive in the morning.

173
00:16:38,560 --> 00:16:47,680
That's a very different persona than your 50-year-old woman that's, you know, struggling with, you know, sleep related issues around menopause, right?

174
00:16:47,840 --> 00:16:51,200
Which there's a whole kind of body of science around and what you can do.

175
00:16:51,360 --> 00:17:05,760
So very different personas and yes in the funnel.nel you can get into customizing different parts of the experience, but if you can really tie the funnel to the user audience that you're reaching, you can just reach a lot more people and let the algorithm just find those people for you.

176
00:17:06,160 --> 00:17:07,840
Last question, pricing and packaging.

177
00:17:08,240 --> 00:17:13,120
Are you using different pricing and packaging on the web versus the app?

178
00:17:13,440 --> 00:17:24,000
And then even on specific funnels or specific customizations, are you showing them different offerings, like only monthly, monthly and annual, annual only lifetime?

179
00:17:24,560 --> 00:17:30,000
How are you thinking about pricing and packaging on the web versus app and then even with individual funnels?

180
00:17:30,480 --> 00:17:31,680
Yes, so the answer is for sure.

181
00:17:31,840 --> 00:17:43,520
And before I even say that., I would say the building relationship is incredibly important because when you move away from, hey, letting Apple handle all the subscription stuff, the reality is Apple does solve a lot of problems that you don't have to think about.

182
00:17:43,840 --> 00:17:56,320
And you can argue whether that's worth thirty percent or something else, but Apple does solve the problem for users and users know how that works when you are on the web, that's your problem now and you have the trust of the customer.

